ures  in my yale campus course as I usually do  and we'll use the recorded lectures  online with quizzes interspersed that  I'm building for the Coursera MOOC the  quizzes though could also voluntarily be  taken by Yale students and I think  actually there might be some who would  find that helpful and they would also  have this Yale students would also have  access to the kind of critical thinking  questions the quote that I  creating also for the for the MOOC and i  think that the feedback that we would  get from both of those parties could be  quite revelatory and i look forward to  seeing what that will be and i think it  could be helpful not only to me  personally but to the progress that we  make over time with these kinds of  explorations there will be online  discussion forums in both places with  some some opportunity for voluntary  interaction between the two groups I  haven't sorted out exactly how I'm going  to do that yet and I think it will  probably be voluntary for students at  Yale but I'm hoping that there will be  some will be interested enough to  participate in that and then as the the  fifth I'd the fifth item the other one  of the two big ideas that I'm talking  about today in order to enhance and  broaden the educational experience I am  involving an international team of  community moderators who come not only  from the United States but also from  countries that were part of the  Mediterranean wide Roman Empire and will  be able to provide timely on the spot  observations I had that experience when  I was doing all learn and was teaching a  course on Roman women I had an online  instructor was helping me do that and  she was leading the sections but every  so often I would come on and interact  and it was amazing because we had people  all over the world taking it at the same  time and if an issue came up for example  about something in Rome a detail about  something the person who happened to be  the related to someone who worked at the  e